A||||27||FieroGT||Need help, and Dr. Ruth is not available!||||9||
Z||000000||FieroGT||06-06-2000||02:25 PM||||Well, guys, I know that this may seem a little out of place on an automotive UBB, but I really have nowhere else to go with this. for the record, I just hit my 29th birthday 2 weeks ago, so this is not a high school matter. <P>I just broke up with a long time GF (about 4 years) about 12 days ago. This was one of those relationships where you know that things haven't been working out for the last year, and it just took this long to realize that your on your way out. Well, I guess that I wasn't about to give up the Fieros and the motorcycles because she wanted me to. Started giving me ultimatums and such, and wanted to get married and have kids ASAP. She is divorced and already has a 6 year old girl who can't stand to see me around her Mom. <P>After the breakup, gathered with some really good friends of 10+ years and had a realy great Memorial Day weekend. Only downfall was losing a lower front balljoint on the GT in the middle of a turn, but all is well with that now. (Only damage was the balljoint itself!)<P>I had been hanging around with my friend's younger sister (23 yrs old.) for a few months (as friends) and last night we took our relationship past the "friends" stage. This girl is really nice to me, treats me like gold, has worked on cars with me, loves to take rides on the bike, and loves the Fiero more than I do. Likes other cars as well, and enjoys spending time with me. I had already talked with her brother about the possibility of me and his sister having a relationship and if he felt comfortable with it, and I know he is telling me the truth when he says it's OK with him. <P>Does this sound like a recipie for disaster? This girl actually goes out of her way to be nice to me and it's something that I guess I'm not used to. She is not clingy, and let's me go out with my friends as I like. She even mentioned that she wanted to go with me to a strip club! I ask myself is this for real??? <P>Anyway, sorry about the post, but you guys seem to be the only people who I can speak freely to at the moment. <P>By the way, everyone, check your balljoints out very well. It is not fun to lose one while in motion. I feel really lucky, since just an hour before mine broke, I had travelled about an hour at speeds over 80 MPH. If it had let loose there, I might not be talking about this right now. Just something to keep in mind, since all our plastic babies are well over 10 years old at this point.  <P>Take care, all.<P>John||||reg||9
Z||000001||lennyb4||06-06-2000||02:38 PM||||Well im no doctor ruth and i really dont want to butt in but since you asked. I see nothing wrong with the age differance to start with or how long its been since you broke up with your last girlfriend. I mean its totally up to you and her to decide what you both want out of the relationship. I have been with my wife (on and off) since we were 16 (i'll be thirty in august). We got married in 1988 and divorced in 1990 due to my indescretions with a couple of women i used to work with. We were seperated for three years and finally got back together. Her family and friends called her stupid, me a loser (and some other chioce names). Kept telling her that he will never change, yada..yada..yada.. BUT we got remarried on valentines day 1994 and have had our ups and downs but one thing is for sure. That we truely do love each other.(and no i have been and plan on being faithfull the rest of our lives together) The moral of this story is no matter what anybody else thinks or says its ultimately up to you and her to decide on where the future is going to take you. Take what people say with a grain of salt.(or do what i do and tell them to mind there own f**king buisness) If you like her and she likes you, then whats the problem. GO FOR IT...Hell you only live once right? Well ive rambled on enough, hope this helped..||||reg||6
Z||000002||fierospeeder||06-06-2000||03:35 PM||||I would stick with her, but wait till she gets her own fiero, then you know it is love.<P>Go slow with the relationship, and see if it works out when your not talking about fieros and motorcycles.  Sharing the same interests does make you closer to someone, and you wont regret it later in life that you gave up your hobbies just to make someone else happy. The age thing is ify, she is 23, and your 6 years older, are her activities the same as yours, going out etc...? As long as you feel comfortable with it, then it is okay, and dont worry what other people think, if you really love someone, it doesn't matter.  My girl/friend likes dating older guys, and i met one of them online, and he started a fight with me, and i said things to him to make him upset, to get back at him.  She blamed me, for saying stuff, when i told her, he shouldn't have said something to me.  He broke up with her, and i told her she was better off because he wasn't that committed to you.  My dad is 7, i guess, years older then my mom, the relationship is fine except that hes lazy.  Good luck to you, and if she makes you have goosebumps all over, go for it.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000003||GT Bastard||06-06-2000||03:50 PM||||It sounds as if your current situation is a recipe for disaster in the long run.  Sooner or later, you may find yourself tied into a marriage and unhappy. So I guess I'm going to jump on the band-wagon on this one.  Go for the girl that makes you happy.  If you don't do this, you may spend the rest of your life wondering "what if?" Forget about the age difference.  If you're in love, nothing can come between you.  Forget those who stand in your way.  In the end, all we have is each other.<P>-Bastard||||reg||1
Z||000004||MotherGoose||06-06-2000||03:50 PM||||My advice is go for it but, go slow.  Don't rush the relationship.||||reg||1
Z||000005||Raydar||06-06-2000||04:04 PM||||I don't see any problem with the age difference. The older you get, the less the difference will matter, anyway. (This from a 43 year-old.) Your friend probably knows you as well as anybody. Having him "approve" of the relationship should be considered a compliment to your character. <BR>If you're not accustomed to someone being nice to you, that's kinda sad. <BR>Learn to accept it, and treasure it.<BR>It's a *good* thing. <BR>Best of luck, man.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000006||tgowens||06-06-2000||04:22 PM||||FieroGT -- first off, no one needs someone else making ultimatums.......that shows a controlling personality ---- just remember, no one can control you but you. Secondly, you and your friend's younger sister seem to have three things going for you that you did not have with the other one -- 1. personalities more attuned with one another, more things in common - 2. the ability to have freedom in a relationship, in other words, the ability to just be you and be accepted for it (non-controlling), and -- 3. the blessing of an older brother who is protective of his younger sister says a lot!! -- whatever you do tho, don't let HIM down by messing things up with his sister!!<P>Now, these are just my opinions and certainly not advice -- in the end, many people can give advice (and most wouldn't follow their own advice) but that's easy when it is still YOUR decision that has to be made and lived with.<P>(These are all things I have said to MY kids (sorry for the term "kids") as I raised four children as a single parent for 13 years.......the oldest is now 29 and the youngest is 19 -- imagine that, I lived to tell about it!!)<P>Bottom line -- enjoy the friendship you and the young lady have - build on that and take things slowly (or at least a pace you both are comfortable with) and just enjoy life!!<P>(Raydar - I got 10 years on ya!!)||||reg||1
Z||000007||FieroGT||06-06-2000||04:31 PM||||Not to overshadow the well thought out posts by others, I fell that MotherGosse's simple post hits the nail on the head. I need to take a slow, cautious look at everything and thake things in a slow, rational manner. Haven't yet reached that point where you can never go back to just being friends, so I'm still holding out. Good thing is that the girl knows all about me past and I have absolutely nothing to hide, since she's sit in on a few male drinking bouts where EVERYTHING seems to come out. Even those things that we've all done in our youth that me would never consider doing as adults. If you've done it then you know what I'm talking about, and there's no need to explain further   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"> .<P>Raydar, it's not that the past women in my life have never treated me nice, it's just that you can tell the difference when someone  is TRULY concerned with how you are doing and feeling, not just asking to be nice, polite, or make conversation. <P>Soemtimes I think that I'm one of those lone wolves that is destined to be single for his entire life, since I've already had 2 long-term relationships that didn't work out. One thing I do know is that I have a few really good friends, not just acquaintances, that would help see me through any problems I may encounter, just as I've done for them in the past. Can't complain about that. <P>Taking it slow right now seems to be the best  course of action. If I feel the need for action right now I can slam a few 5500 RPM shifts off on the GT and that'll always put a smile on my face. Gotta love that 2-3 shift!<P>GT Bastard - I like the way you think, bro!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>John<p>[This message has been edited by FieroGT (edited 06-06-2000).]||||reg||14
Z||000008||DaRkLoRD||06-06-2000||04:42 PM||||I'm probably the last guy who should try to give advice on relationships, as I've been single for as long as I can remember... It never fails.. every girl I like just wants to be friends...  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"><BR>There are a few girls that like me, but I don't like any of them.. I'm weird, eh?<P>Anyway, the only thing I can say is (warning: corny cliche ahead!) follow your heart. age differences aren't important if you truly care about the person.. the girl I really like is 4 years younger than I am, but the difference isn't apparent.. we act the same.. maybe I'm just immature for my age (19).. either way, it doesn't really matter cause she's going out with someone else anyway..  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000009||FieroGT||06-06-2000||04:42 PM||||Just typed up the previous post and realized that there were other posts after I had last read them! <P>Fierospeeder - Gotta love that one about getting her own Fiero; she has a Saturn coupe right now. At least they're somewhat related! If I could only get her to drive a manual tranny now, that would be great!<P>tgowens - that advice sounds like it's really good. Older brother is really a good friend of mine, only fault he has is that he drives Fords (LOL), but always likes to take my Fiero for a spin! Saya its the closest thing to a go-cart he has ever driven! But yes he approves, and told me that he doesn't want to be in the middle of any problems we might have....two stipulations though. 1) no physical harm and 2) no cheating. since I don't have a history of either of the two (plus I have been tols that I have the pacience of a saint!) I think that we are in good shape. Best thing was to get everything out into the open and let everyone know where I am coming from. That actually made everybody more comfortable, because I always am direct and to the point. I like to be treated the same way. <P>Keep the responses coming, guys & girls!<P>Thanks again for the mature words, everyone. Make a post like this somewhere else and it's Jerry Springer time in cyberspace!<P>Steve (sorry, I can never get the caps order straight on your username!) - You need to hand in there and get the career on its path. 19 is a great age (if I rememember correctly). Just stay away from the credit cards and keep yourself from becoming an involuntary parent and all will be well for you. Give me a holler - I still want to follow up on your website offer. I can't use HTML worth a damn and need all the help I can get! Later bro!<P><BR>John<p>[This message has been edited by FieroGT (edited 06-06-2000).]||||reg||10
Z||000010||tgowens||06-06-2000||04:53 PM||||John, thanks for the compliment -- it sounds like you have your head on straight and are drawing your own conclusions (like I said - it is YOUR decision to make anyway -- just remember a father's words of wisdom - "when a relationship brings you more pain than comfort, it's time to get out" - in other words -- be happy within yourself because of who you are and what you do -- <P>Good luck to you John -- at least you learned one thing today -- there are many others out here who you can talk to -- any time -- who needs Dr. Ruth anyway?? (sorry Dr. Ruth -- oh no, I can see it now -- Dr. Ruth emasculates man because of fatherly advice!!! Film at 11:00 !!)||||reg||1
Z||000011||DaRkLoRD||06-06-2000||05:13 PM||||John,<P>I've had a Visa card for over a year now.. always pay my bills on time.. I have a perfect credit rating in fact.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><BR>(I wonder if I could get a loan to buy a Fiero???????? or maybe I just need a better job. heh)<P>No worries about becoming an involuntary parent.. yet. heh.. Actually.. I'm not sure I ever will.. my family has a history of heart disease.. I'm no different... (my heart is quite screwed up. heh) so my kids would likely have heart problems too... so... until fertility clinics can fix genetic damage like in Gattaca, (I didn't like the movie but the idea of creating engineered kids is cool IMHO) I'll try not to get anyone pregnant.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><BR>(which should be easy since I have no girlfriend.)<P>I'll send ya an email about the web page tonight!||||reg||1
Z||000012||FieroGT||06-06-2000||05:17 PM||||tgowens - you're quite welcome, man!<BR>Having raised those kids and putting them on the path to good is surely a difficult thing and being that you're still among us, shows that you're one hell of a strong person. I admire that attribute!<P>All in all, I'm glad that I took this mental-health day from work (1st time in 8 months, so don't think I'm lazy!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"> ) so I can get some good thinking done. Since it's already after 5:00,I take it that some of you workplace posting-junkies will be going home now. Enjoy the rest of your day and thanks for the words of wisdom.<P>John||||reg||1
Z||000013||FieroGT||06-06-2000||05:28 PM||||Steve - Jsut missed your post again! I really have to learn how to type faster or to limit my posts to single paagraphs.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>Looking forward to seeing your info tonight!<P>Hang in there man, my family doesn't have the greatest track record for hearts, either. As a matter of fact, most males in my family don't see past 67. Scary, but I can't live my life in fear of that fact. I guess now that I'm approaching 30, I have made some strong commitments to my personal well-being, like quitting smoking, and possibly joining a health club. I feel physically fine, but then again, to use an analogy, the motor in your car always runs fine until it quits running, doesn't it? I want to make sure that I get what's coming to me from life, now that I have reached the age where I realized I am not immortal  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">  ! Take care of yourself, man!<P>everyone - this is really a great forum! Does Cliff have some kind of idiot-filter that blocks out undesireable IP addresses or something?<P>John<BR>||||reg||14
Z||000014||batboy||06-06-2000||08:55 PM||||With the lousy luck I have at relationships, take my advice with a grain of salt (maybe even with the whole salt shaker). Be careful with those rebound relationships, sometimes they can cause more pain then the one you just left. I'm all for trying to be friends first, before you get too involved, hopefully you two already are friends from the sounds of it. I recommend these things: honesty, being true, communication, respect for each other, and trust. Without those qualities on both sides, you're doomed. In the way distant past, I once dated a lady who was 7 years older than me and one who was 11 years younger than me. Age was not an issue either time...it's more the maturity factor. Like others have mentioned, taking it slow and easy is wise.||||reg||1
Z||000015||Boodoggin||06-06-2000||09:40 PM||||Send her emails at work, just telling her how beutifull you think she is.  Girls love that.||||reg||12
Z||000016||tgowens||06-06-2000||10:07 PM||||good idea Boodoggin - or, go to my site and send her a Fiero postcard!!!........ <A HREF="http://www.tgowens.com/fiero/postcards.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.tgowens.com/fiero/postcards.html</A> ||||reg||1
Z||000017||GT Bastard||06-06-2000||10:11 PM||||I dunno about you guys, but I think it's time we get input from one of our forum ladies....   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000018||batboy||06-07-2000||12:56 AM||||Personally, I'd really like hearing from some of the SINGLE forum ladies, but I don't think we have any that are unattached. Single Fiero ladies are in high demand apparently.||||reg||1
